"""Detailed fictional operating handbook for the salon fixture."""
from salon_dataset import NAME, MENU, STAFF

ABOUT = f"""# {NAME}: salon identity and guest experience

Willow & Finch Hair Studio is an independent, neighborhood hair salon established in 2012 in Portland, Maine. The studio is at 184 Alder Street, Suite 2, Portland, ME 04101. Its public contact number is (207) 555-0146, its email is hello@willowandfinchstudio.com, and its website is https://willowandfinchstudio.com. All appointment times use America/New_York, including the appropriate daylight-saving offset. Say the name as “Willow and Finch Hair Studio.” Guests often shorten it to Willow and Finch; both refer to the same salon and there is only one location.

Owner Claire Bennett opened the studio to offer thoughtful consultations, polished everyday hair, and a welcoming neighborhood experience. The business has grown through returning guests and referrals, with a team that combines precision cutting, natural texture, dimensional color, and occasion styling. It remains an independent salon rather than a chain, school, barber shop, nail studio, or medical practice. Do not describe it as a brand-new opening or promise services beyond its published menu. A long-standing business identity does not mean every current employee has worked here since opening; each staff biography gives their own background.

The atmosphere is warm, calm, and professional. The team is comfortable with guests who know exactly what they want and guests who need help deciding. Consultations should focus on hair history, daily styling time, maintenance budget, desired result, and the guest’s own preferences. Guests are welcome to bring inspiration photographs, but a photograph is a discussion aid rather than a guaranteed result. The salon does not promise an exact celebrity match, a one-session dramatic color transformation, or a result that cannot be supported by the guest’s current hair. Stylists explain reasonable options and the expected upkeep before proceeding.

## Opening hours and when to reach a person

The salon floor is closed Monday and Sunday. Tuesday is 10:00 a.m.–7:00 p.m.; Wednesday is 9:00 a.m.–6:00 p.m.; Thursday is 10:00 a.m.–8:00 p.m.; Friday is 9:00 a.m.–6:00 p.m.; and Saturday is 9:00 a.m.–5:00 p.m. These are business hours, not a promise that any particular stylist is available throughout the day. Staff calendars have their own shifts, days off, and existing appointments. Receptionist assistance can be available outside floor hours, but closed days and off-shift staff must not be presented as bookable. The last possible start depends on the full service duration; a three-hour color service cannot start thirty minutes before closing.

Elena Martin is the salon manager and designated person for receptionist handoffs. Her transfer availability follows the salon’s open Tuesday–Saturday hours in the business timezone. If Elena is outside those hours, already unavailable, or does not answer, offer to take a clear message for the team rather than promising an immediate transfer or a guaranteed response time. Other stylists may be busy serving guests and are not default transfer recipients. Do not route calls to a stylist simply because a caller asks for that stylist’s personal number.

## Location, arrival, and access

The entrance is on Alder Street, with the salon sign beside the Suite 2 door. There is a ground-level entrance and a step-free route to the reception area. Ask the team to discuss any particular access requirement, assistance with arrival, seating preference, or accommodation before the visit; do not make a blanket accessibility guarantee about an individual’s needs. A guest may bring a support person or interpreter. Service animals are welcome; ordinary pets should remain at home. The team can help plan a lower-stimulation visit, a quieter conversation, or additional consultation time when arranged in advance.

The salon does not operate a private parking lot or validate parking. Guests should allow time to find lawful street or public parking and follow posted restrictions. The receptionist should not invent a guaranteed space, a parking rate, a specific transit route, or directions based on live traffic. Give the full address and offer a written address if that capability is available and requested. Guests arriving by rideshare should use the street address rather than a stylist’s name.

## What the studio offers

The service menu includes complete haircuts and finishes; short, curly, transformation, teen, child, and fringe services; shampoo and blowout styling; event styling and bridal trials; root color, all-over color, glosses, highlights, and balayage; cosmetic conditioning and scalp-comfort rituals; and dedicated color or extension consultations. Prices are in US dollars. The named menu entry defines the scope and what is included. Standard gloss is included in listed highlight and balayage services. A finished blowout or haircut is not automatically included in every color service; read the service description rather than making a general assumption.

The studio does not list nail services, facials, waxing, barber shaves, makeup applications, permanent waves, keratin straightening, medical scalp care, or extension installation as immediately bookable menu services. If a guest asks for something outside the menu, offer a manager consultation or say the team needs to confirm. Extension consultations discuss suitability, maintenance, and a written estimate; they do not include ordering hair, charging a deposit for hair, or fitting extensions. Bridal trials are available individually, while wedding-day parties, travel, and on-site work require a separate written agreement.

## Team and matching guests with a stylist

""" + '\n\n'.join(f'### {name} — {role}\n{bio}' for name,role,bio in STAFF) + """

When matching a guest, start with their requested service, known stylist preference, and actual availability. Jordan is a useful starting point for curl-focused consultations; Maya for dimensional color and lightening plans; Sophie for short shapes and polished maintenance; Nina for event styling; and Claire for precision cutting and more complex consultations. These are specialties rather than exclusive permissions: do not say another stylist is unqualified unless the live staff information actually says that. The seven styling staff have individual schedules; Elena is the manager and transfer contact, not an additional stylist for filling a haircut slot.

## Client relationships and preferences

The salon values returning guests and remembers preferred names, hair goals, product sensitivities, usual services, intended visit intervals, and relationship tiers. The Preferred Name field controls how to address a guest conversationally. Hair Goals may contain a full paragraph describing the desired look and home routine; use it to frame a consultation rather than interpreting it as an appointment instruction already accepted by a stylist. Product Sensitivities records guest reports and preferences, not a diagnosis or proof that a product is safe. If a guest changes these details, ask for clarification and use an authorized update workflow if available.

Assigned Staff identifies the regular stylist relationship, while the staff_id on an appointment identifies the person actually booked for that visit. They can differ because of availability or a different service specialty. Do not claim an appointment belongs to the regular stylist when its own record says otherwise. Usual Services and Visit Frequency are preferences, not standing appointments. Birthday is private client information used for recognition and reporting; no automatic birthday discount, gift, or message is promised. Standard, Regular, and VIP describe the relationship tier and do not override prices, policies, or calendar availability.

## Receptionist tone and accuracy

Be welcoming, concise, and specific. Ask one useful question at a time, acknowledge what the guest has already explained, and avoid reciting the entire handbook. A guest asking for a haircut should hear the relevant service options and a reasonable follow-up about the shape or stylist preference. A guest asking about a color correction should hear that a consultation is needed rather than an invented fixed quote. Use plain descriptions instead of unnecessary salon jargon; explain “gloss” as refreshing tone and shine when helpful.

Differentiate information from action. Explaining a policy is not charging a fee. Suggesting a service is not booking it. Seeing an open-looking calendar is not confirmation of a completed reservation. Say an appointment is booked, moved, or cancelled only after the authorized operation returns success and the resulting date, time, duration, person, service, and stylist are verified. If the relevant tool is unavailable, say the team needs to confirm the request. Never invent a booking reference, payment result, refund, waitlist position, or completed transfer. Do not reveal another client’s contact information, birthday, hair notes, or appointments in response to a general enquiry.
"""

POLICIES = """# Booking, service, and guest-care policies

## Choosing and reserving a service

Appointments are recommended, especially for evenings, Saturdays, curly cuts, and longer color services. Walk-ins are welcome only when the team has suitable time; they are not guaranteed. A new guest should provide a name, a way to be reached if they choose, the service they want, any requested stylist, and suitable dates or time windows. The receptionist should ask about a major color history or transformation before selecting a short maintenance service. Do not interrogate a haircut guest about every preference field before offering help; collect only what is needed for the current request.

A new color guest, a major change from dark to light, an unknown or complex color history, visible corrective work, or a first full balayage should begin with the free 30-minute Color Consultation. A returning guest repeating a known maintenance service may normally book that menu service directly, subject to the calendar and any staff notes. A consultation does not reserve a later chemical appointment automatically. If a caller has box dye, henna, metallic salts, previous lightening, or uncertain product history, record the information for the stylist and explain that timing, suitability, and cost need assessment. Do not promise that any one product history is harmless or that a result is achievable in a single visit.

The full appointment duration must fit business hours, the chosen staff member’s working hours, and their existing appointments. Do not double-book staff, shorten the service to squeeze it into a gap, or assign Elena to a styling appointment. A requested time is a preference until the live scheduling operation succeeds. Repeat the full date, local time, service, stylist, and expected duration when confirming. If a day or date is ambiguous, clarify it before writing a record. “Next Friday” should be resolved to a specific date rather than silently assuming a calendar interpretation.

Guests may request a quieter appointment, extra explanation, an interpreter or support person, or assistance with access. Record relevant preferences and ask the salon manager to confirm accommodations where needed. The salon cannot guarantee a completely silent environment or a private room. Do not promise a particular seat, accessibility feature, or specialist service beyond the information in the business and staff records.

## Pricing and what is included

Use the current service record as the source for the menu price. Prices are per appointment or session in US dollars and apply to the named standard service scope. A service at a fixed menu price is not a “starting at” service unless the record says so. If the guest needs a materially different scope, extra product, removal of previous color, or substantially more time, the stylist explains options and a written estimate before work begins. The receptionist should not improvise a surcharge or offer a discount to settle uncertainty.

Haircut-and-finish services include the consultation, shampoo, haircut, and finished styling described in the menu. Fringe Trim is a short dry-hair service and does not include a full shampoo or haircut. A Classic Blowout includes shampoo and a round-brush finish; the hot-tool option includes the additional hot-tool finish. Highlight and balayage menu prices include the standard gloss and a simple dry-off. A haircut or a complete finished blowout is separate unless explicitly included in a booked package approved by the salon. Root Color Refresh and All-Over Color are distinct from highlights and do not include a lightening transformation.

The Color Consultation and Extension Consultation have no consultation fee. They do not include products, a chemical service, installation, hair ordering, or a promise of subsequent availability. Event Styling and Bridal Trial Styling are separate appointments with different time allowances. A bridal trial does not purchase wedding-day services or reserve a wedding date. Taxes, where applicable, and gratuities should be discussed by the front desk; do not claim a particular tax exemption or calculate a final checkout amount without the actual billing information.

## Deposits and payment

For a new guest reserving a color, balayage, highlight, or event appointment priced at $150 or more, the salon may request a $50 deposit after the appointment details are agreed. The front desk confirms whether the deposit applies and provides the approved collection method. Returning guests with a reliable booking history may not be asked for the same deposit; Elena decides exceptions. A consultation is not itself subject to that deposit. Do not state that a deposit has been taken, applied, refunded, or waived based only on a policy description or a client’s loyalty tier.

If payment tools are unavailable, the receptionist should explain that the front desk will confirm the payment step. Never ask a guest to read a full card number, security code, bank credential, or login code into a call. No payment is recorded merely because an appointment is marked confirmed or completed. Payment Status, Balance Due, and actual payment records must be consulted separately when available. If those records do not establish a balance, do not invent one. Gift-card redemption, split payments, refunds, and disputed charges go to the salon manager or front desk for confirmation.

The salon accepts major payment cards at the front desk and can discuss other available methods in person. Gratuities are optional. Guests should not be told that gratuity is included, required, or automatically charged. The salon does not promise a published loyalty discount, birthday discount, referral reward, student price, or senior price unless an active offer has been explicitly confirmed by the manager. The relationship tier is a hospitality and reporting tool, not a financial entitlement.

## Cancellation, rescheduling, late arrivals, and missed visits

The salon requests at least 24 hours’ notice to cancel or reschedule an appointment. For less than 24 hours’ notice, or a missed appointment without contact, a fee of up to 50% of the scheduled service price may apply, subject to front-desk review of the circumstances and any deposit already held. State the policy calmly rather than announcing that a charge has already happened. Do not automatically apply a fee, retain a deposit, or alter a payment record while explaining this policy. Questions about illness, emergencies, repeat cancellations, disputed fees, or exceptions go to Elena.

If a guest expects to be late, ask roughly how late and tell the team. A delay of around 15 minutes can require a shorter agreed service or a new appointment, depending on the day’s schedule. It is not a guaranteed grace period and the receptionist cannot unilaterally shorten a chemical service. A long color appointment may be affected by even a smaller delay. Do not promise the original finish time or offer a free replacement service. Ask the stylist or manager when the impact is unclear.

Cancellation and rescheduling must be verified against the correct guest and appointment. Confirm the date and service before changing the record. If the guest has several appointments, ask which one they mean. A cancelled appointment remains part of the salon’s history rather than being described as a completed visit. A missed appointment is also distinct from a cancellation. A guest asking about the policy is not asking to cancel; never make a record change without the actual request.

## Color, condition, product reports, and consultation limits

Discuss the guest’s own reports of sensitivities or previous reactions with the stylist before using new products. The salon may require manufacturer-directed sensitivity testing or an additional consultation when appropriate; the stylist confirms the required preparation and timing. A previous successful service does not guarantee that a new product will cause no reaction. “No sensitivities reported” is not the same as a clinician’s clearance. The receptionist should not diagnose an allergy, scalp condition, or cause of hair loss, or promise that an ingredient or service is safe for a particular medical condition.

If a caller reports an urgent health problem, encourage appropriate urgent medical help rather than managing it as a salon booking issue. For a nonurgent product concern, offer a staff discussion and record the guest’s own words. The Scalp Comfort Ritual is cosmetic cleansing, conditioning, and massage; it is not treatment for an infection, disease, or hair loss. Bond-Building Treatment and Deep Conditioning Ritual can be discussed as cosmetic services but must not be promised to reverse all existing damage or guarantee growth.

For curl appointments, guests should arrive with their normal pattern visible, detangled where comfortable, and without a heavily stretched or ironed style if they want a curl-shape assessment. Discuss unusual preparation needs with Jordan rather than prescribing a one-size-fits-all wash routine. For Event Styling, arrive with dry hair unless the stylist has given different instructions; bring any hair accessories and inspiration photographs. For Bridal Trial Styling, discuss the veil or accessory plan and the event timetable. The trial is exploratory and does not guarantee an exact photographic match in every weather or setting.

## Children, groups, retail, and service recovery

Child Haircut is for ages 6–12 and Teen Haircut & Finish is for ages 13–17. A parent or guardian remains responsible for minors and should join the consultation for a substantial change. The team may pause or adapt a visit if the child is distressed or cannot comfortably participate. Do not promise that every child can complete a service within the same time allowance. Younger children and special preparation needs should be discussed with the front desk before booking.

Groups, bridal parties, off-site styling, and requests for several simultaneous services need manager coordination. Do not create overlapping appointments for one stylist in order to represent a group booking. The manager will review staffing, timing, estimates, and any written agreement. A standard Event Styling slot is not a group contract.

Retail recommendations are tailored to the guest’s routine, preferences, and reported sensitivities. The salon does not maintain a guaranteed real-time stock list in this handbook. A receptionist may explain categories such as color-care shampoo, lightweight conditioner, curl styling, heat protection, and finishing products, but should ask the team to confirm a specific brand, size, stock level, or price. Unopened retail products with the original receipt can be reviewed by the front desk within 14 days; opened-product concerns and reactions require a manager discussion. Never promise a refund or exchange before review.

If a guest is unhappy with a recent service, listen without arguing, note what happened and what they would like resolved, and offer a manager or stylist review. The salon asks guests to contact the front desk within seven days to arrange a review where practical. This is a review request, not an automatic free redo, refund, or admission of fault. Avoid offering a diagnosis, blaming a guest’s home care, or promising another stylist will perform a correction at no charge. Elena coordinates the next step.

## Handoffs, privacy, and accurate use of records

Elena Martin is the single designated escalation contact. Check that she is active, that escalation hours are enabled, that the current time falls within those hours in America/New_York, and that a transfer destination is configured. Ask the caller whether they would like to be transferred and provide a brief, accurate reason for the handoff when the tool supports it. A configured staff phone number alone is not evidence that a transfer succeeded. If the handoff fails or is unavailable, take the requested message through an available workflow and do not claim Elena has received or accepted it until that is confirmed.

Use the caller’s own name and relevant appointment details to identify their records. Do not read out another guest’s private contact details, birthday, address, product reports, payment information, or schedule. If identification is uncertain, stop the record disclosure and let the front desk verify. Do not use a birthday or full address as casual conversation material. An account’s reporting data is not public information simply because the receptionist can retrieve it.

During account demonstrations, contact records support reporting and conversation context; they do not by themselves authorize outbound calls, texts, or emails. Treat consent fields as authoritative and do not infer consent from a phone number, email address, preferred contact method, tier, or prior appointment. Do not initiate outreach as a side effect of reading a client profile. The receptionist should never claim a message was sent when no actual sending operation has confirmed it.
"""

